---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import WaitlistForm from "../../components/WaitlistForm.astro";
import Tile from "../../components/Tile.astro";
---
<Base title="VLE — lifecycle (planned) · velocit.ee" description="VLE — Velocitee Lifecycle Engine. Drift detection, auto-documentation, single-command repair. Planned phase 4.">
  <section>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-wrap px-6 lg:px-10 py-16">
      <a href="/engines" class="text-2xs text-muted hover:text-cream transition-colors font-mono">← engines</a>
      <div class="mt-4 flex items-baseline gap-3">
        <span class="text-2xs text-muted font-medium tracking-widest">PHASE 4 · PLANNED</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="mt-2 font-mono text-5xl md:text-6xl text-cream tracking-tightest leading-[1] flex items-baseline gap-4 flex-wrap">
        <span>VLE</span>
        <span class="text-muted text-2xl md:text-3xl">— lifecycle</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-[60ch] font-sans font-light text-text leading-relaxed">
        Keep an existing velocitee deployment in shape over time. <strong class="text-cream font-medium">Drift detection</strong>
        against your <code class="text-green-hi">velocitee.yml</code> intent.
        <strong class="text-cream font-medium">Auto-generated docs</strong> of the actually-running stack.
        <strong class="text-cream font-medium">Single-command repair</strong> when reality diverges from intent.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-y border-border bg-surface/30">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-wrap px-6 lg:px-10 py-14">
      <p class="label">// design directions</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-mono text-2xl text-cream tracking-tightest">what VLE will probably do.</h2>
      <ul class="mt-7 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <Tile title="drift report" status="muted">
          <p class="text-muted text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed">
            Run on a schedule. Compare the running infrastructure to the recorded
            <code>velocitee.yml</code> + handoff manifests. Produce a structured report — what changed, who changed
            it (if known), what to do about it.
          </p>
        </Tile>
        <Tile title="documentation generator" status="muted">
          <p class="text-muted text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed">
            Render your stack as a living document — diagrams, runbooks, on-call references. Pulled from
            manifests + live API state, not from a wiki page nobody updates.
          </p>
        </Tile>
        <Tile title="repair shop" status="muted">
          <p class="text-muted text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed">
            For drift VLE knows how to fix: re-run the relevant engine, with the right manifests, in the
            right order. The same engines you’d run by hand — orchestrated.
          </p>
        </Tile>
        <Tile title="renewal checks" status="muted">
          <p class="text-muted text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed">
            TLS certificates, OS update windows, container image freshness, password rotations. All in the
            same drift report; all repairable from the same single command.
          </p>
        </Tile>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-8 text-2xs text-muted font-mono max-w-[60ch] leading-relaxed">
        VLE is the furthest-out engine — VME and VNE come first, VSE next. The shape will become real once
        we have enough VSE deployments running long enough to know what actually drifts.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-wrap px-6 lg:px-10 py-14">
      <p class="label">// stay informed</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-mono text-2xl text-cream tracking-tightest max-w-[28ch] leading-[1.1]">
        we'll send one email when VLE work begins.
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-6 max-w-[44ch]">
        <WaitlistForm />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
